going out by the chair of the Health  Committee for Health Secretary Jeremy  Hunt you saw him there what did you  learn this morning well I think we  learnt some very important new bits of  information first of all that the virus  has now moved from the contained stage  to what the government calls the delay  stage the second stage and they are  doing very active preparations for the  final stage known as the mitigate stage  and what that really means is that we  have got to get our heads around the  possibility of potentially 20% of the  population getting this virus that's the  level of penetration in Hebei Province  in China the epicenter of the virus and  so the governor is having to make some  very difficult decisions first of all  they say this morning they're going to  bring back they're going to allow  medical students who ordinarily wouldn't  be able to operate as doctors until they  qualified if they're in their final year  they might be allowed to practice as  doctors and first-year foundation year  doctors also would be allowed to do  things they previously might not have  been allowed to do that's a very  significant step and some big debates  going on inside government about school  closures as well do you think the NHS  can cope with 20% of the country getting  coronavirus well no Health System  anywhere in the world can cope with a  pandemic in a way that means it's  completely business as usual and  professor which he was very open about  the fact that there may be some impact  even on urgent treatment we asked him  what it would mean for example for  cancer patients but what he said was  that the the impact at his height would  be for about three weeks and so if you  get the timing of that three weeks right  if you're able to predict accurately  when that three weeks starts and when it  ends then you can do an enormous amount  to make sure that urgent treatment is  protected either because it happens  before that or because it happens after  that  but yes there are going to be you know  impacts particularly for people who have  pneumonia and respiratory diseases where  the pressures will be at their highest  it doesn't help though does it that that  we have 43 thousand fewer nurses in the  NHS  we need what we do need more nurses we  need more doctors that's the only long  term where we're going to reduce the  pressures on the NHS the government's  got plans for that but I think it's  important to say that you know the scale  of what may happen means that there's  going to be pressure in health systems  all over the world and most people look  at the NHS in fact there was an  international study an independent study  done by Johns Hopkins University amongst  others in the United States that said  that the UK had the second best prepared  health system in the world for dealing  with pandemics and most countries that  don't have an NHS would think this is  one of the moments when it's very very  good to have one but the government is  now changing its policy towards nursing  and students isn't it it's it's funding  5,000 more student training places it's  giving nursing students 5,000 a year to  help get staff to come back in do you  accept that cutting bursaries when you  were in charge was now an error well I  never wanted to do that and I I fought  against it but I was told by the  Treasury if I wanted an extra billion  for the NHS that was going to have to be  part of the deal so I'm very pleased  that policy has been changed and you  know we have increased nurse training  places by a quarter there are the plans  for 50,000 nurses these are the only  long-term wave that we're going to  reduce pressure on the NHS front line  it's more doctors and more nurses but  the vacancy rates are still really high  15% and London 16% for some mental  health units that's why we we urgently  need these new 50,000 nurses that the  government's promising what what are you  doing to protect yourself from  coronavirus well I'm listening very  carefully to the the advice I'm not a  doctor myself so I'm listening to what  the the doctors are telling us and we  heard some very important information  from Professor Witte at the Select  Committee hearing that for example the  virus can potentially survive on a hard  service for up to three days it's  unlikely to survive that long  so it is incredibly important to wash  your hands when you arrive from work or  when you arrive home at the end of the  day if you've been on public transport  and you know catching your sneeze in the  tissue thinning the tissue washing your  hands regularly these are the things  that will have the most impact in  slowing the spread of the virus what  about travel have you cancelled any  travel plans well I was due to go to a  conference in the United States this  weekend  that was actually canceled by the  organizers and I think there are gonna  be all sorts of short-term impacts that  we start to see obviously people are  very worried after the news from fly B  this morning and I know that the  judgment call for the government is how  you minimize the economic and social  disruption whilst always making sure  you're taking the decisions necessary to  save people's lives why do you think  that our government's view on things  like closing schools is it's so markedly  different from Italy's well they are in  a different phase of the virus it's it's  much more advanced initially than it is  here but we had a very good discussion  at the Select Committee this morning  about that and this is now a live issue  for ministers to decide and the reason  that it's a difficult judgement is  because coronavirus doesn't seem to  affect children as badly as vulnerable  older people but it seems like they can  catch it and pass it on so in that  context you might want to close a school  to slow down the spread of a virus but  if you do that you're then  meaning-making  meaning that mums and dads might not be  able to go to work 